+The breadth count is a single split: on seed~0, windowed-CQR reaches at least 0.85 coverage in 18 of 19 runs. Search-R1 leaves about 24 test prefixes per seed, which is why its standard deviations reach 0.10--0.17. Coverage alone does not distinguish a useful interval from a trivially wide one, so width and unbounded fraction are reported throughout; Figure~\ref{fig:case_study} also shows the price of calibration, intervals about one and a half times as wide as the agent's, which costs nothing when the question is whether the run still fits and matters when a tight point estimate is needed. Censored capacity and cost results are measured against an interpolated label. Finally, savings and Cost-of-Pass are counterfactual replays: they assume adding the guard would not change agent behaviour and that the logged sample is representative.
 
 \paragraph{Broader impact.}
 The intended effect is to reduce wasted compute, energy, and money on agent runs that cannot succeed; the Warehouse gate at $\delta{=}0.10$ illustrates that potential in one environment (86.3\% of doomed-run spend avoided in replay, without using remaining-budget intervals). The corresponding risk is that a stopping threshold set too aggressively terminates runs that would have finished; $\delta$ calibration exists to bound that risk at a user-chosen level, and the counterfactual caveat above means a deployer should not treat the reported savings as a guaranteed live return when choosing $\delta$, nor assume token environments will see Warehouse-scale savings.
